WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.820
- [Narrator] Oh, the seashore.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:07.220
The waves, the water, the seaweed,

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:08.063
the sheep?

00:00:09.461 --> 00:00:11.580
Are they eating seaweed?
(sheep bleating)

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.430
We've gotta figure this out.

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:16.263
(mid-tempo music)

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.380
So, here's a little context
to start off the story.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.290
We're on an island, high,
high, high up in Scotland,

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:27.800
called North Ronaldsay.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.080
It's so remote you can only
get here by ferry or airplane.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.150
And on this small, picturesque island,

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:35.840
home to only 50 people,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.063
lives a very, very rare breed
of sheep: the seaweed sheep.

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:45.773
Meet Kevin, the authority
on all-things seaweed sheep.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.680
- [Narrator] So let's learn
more about this rare species.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.757
Welcome to Seaweed Sheep 101.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.253
(sheep bleats)

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.060
- [Narrator] They are smaller
than the average sheep,

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.140
and their wool can be
black, white, gray or brown.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.640
The males have big, curly horns.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.467
And so do some females, and ...

00:01:27.111 --> 00:01:29.611
(light music)

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.220
- [Narrator] As a result,
the sheep were confined

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.630
to the seashore and were
forced to survive on seaweed.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:44.320
This changed their
digestive system over time,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.960
making them one of only
two animals in the world

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.510
who are able to survive on seaweed alone.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.810
And this wall, known as the sheep dyke,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.210
makes sure they stay on
the shore. Right, Kevin?

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.390
- [Narrator] Oh, OK, well,
Heather, over to you.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.370
- Behind me is the native sheep dyke.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.810
This was constructed in 1832.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.710
The North Ronaldsay
sheep dyke is essential

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:12.010
in the genetic structure of the sheep.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.170
North Ronaldsay sheep have
a very high intolerance

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:17.920
to copper, so they die from over-nutrition

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.850
by grazing on the grass for too long.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.030
So we keep them on the
shore, and they eat seaweed,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.907
which has got very low
levels of iodine and copper.

00:02:25.915 --> 00:02:28.496
(sheep bleating)

00:02:28.496 --> 00:02:30.913
(soft music)

00:02:51.023 --> 00:02:53.440
(soft music)

00:02:57.412 --> 00:03:00.412
(electronic music)

